Yes—and the technically interesting word is antlers, not horns. Reindeer are deer, so they periodically build, mineralize, use, shed, and then rebuild an entire branched bony structure. Antlers are true bone, and while growing they are wrapped in vascularized “velvet” that supplies nutrients and minerals. Deer-family antlers are among the fastest-growing mammalian tissues known. 

That gives us a very useful biological pattern:

temporary scaffold → extreme vascular supply → rapid patterned growth → mineralization → functional deployment → programmed detachment → regeneration

The remarkable part is that the animal does not maintain the expensive structure permanently. It keeps the instructions and regenerative machinery, then reconstructs the hardware when the seasonal payoff justifies it. Related cervids demonstrate how extreme this can be: Yellowstone elk can complete antler growth in roughly 90–140 days, with mature bulls adding around two-thirds of an inch per day during the fastest phase; Alaska moose can add up to about half a pound of antler per day during a roughly four-month growth period. 

That suggests several transferable operators:

Store blueprint, not structure. Sometimes maintaining an asset continuously is more expensive than retaining the capability to regenerate it.

Burst-build architecture. Resources can be concentrated into an extraordinarily fast construction window rather than distributed evenly through time.

Soft-build → hard-lock. During construction the system stays vascular, nourished, responsive, and relatively plastic; once the geometry is finished, it shuts down that support layer and hardens the result.

Scheduled obsolescence can be adaptive. Shedding isn’t necessarily failure. It can remove damaged or metabolically unnecessary hardware while creating the opportunity for a revised rebuild.

And the deepest one may be:

Regeneration means the final object isn’t the real asset. The generative process is.

The antler is disposable. The ability to make an antler is persistent.

That belongs directly beside your broader pattern of distinguishing surface artifacts from the machinery capable of reconstructing them.